working_notes/canonical_summary.md:3:The original essential-sup tail condition
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:8:is a strong sufficient condition, not a necessary process-wise characterization as stated. In fact, under a finite-horizon known exogenous law, a horizon-aware oracle learner can get regret at most \(T/K\) by maximizing the conditional reward curve over a \(K\)-grid at each time. Taking \(K=T\) gives \(O(1)\) regret for every known exogenous law, regardless of hitting-tail behavior.
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:29:Thus sublinear uniform regret follows if there are schedules \(H_T,K_T\) with \((H_T+1)\log K_T=o(T)\), \(T/K_T=o(T)\), and uniform truncation loss \(o(T)\). For power tails \(\Delta_T(H)\le T H^{-\alpha}\), the existing proof gives rate \(T^{(\alpha+1)/(2\alpha+1)}(\log T)^{\alpha/(2\alpha+1)}\) with \(K=T\).
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:33:Iteration 3 sharpened this lower-bound side into a Bayes-gap certificate. For unknown exogenous classes, if a prior over laws leaves the learner unable to identify which conditional reward curve is active before many decisions, then Bayes/Yao averaging lower-bounds minimax regret by the comparator value minus the best posterior-mixture quote value. In the identical-prefix terminal case this gives
working_notes/canonical_summary.md:37:A \(q\)-scaled hidden-terminal pair gives regret at least \(qD/8\) at horizon \(T=D+1\), and, with absorbing post-horizon prices, has \(\beta(h)=q\) for \(h<D\) and truncation loss \(\asymp q(D-H)\). This is a useful lower-bound atom, but not a matching power-tail rate theorem: under a power envelope \(q\lesssim D^{-\alpha}\), the one-block lower bound is only \(\Omega(D^{1-\alpha})\).
