iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:62:Separately, the clean tail upper bound remains:
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:77:1. Known-law oracle: every known finite-horizon exogenous law has \(R_T\le T/K\), hence \(O(1)\) with \(K=T\). So no nontrivial fixed-law tail condition is necessary.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:79:2. Finite-grid minimax duality: the attainable reward set \(C\subset\mathbb R^\Theta\), finite-dimensional minimax, and fixed-prior Bayes myopic optimality prove the exact finite-class identity above.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:86:4. Reward-measure domination: if all \(\mu^\theta_{t,a}\) are dominated by \(\lambda_t\), then \(q_t\in L^\infty(\lambda_t;\Delta(G))\), weak-* compactness gives product compactness of \(C\), and finite-subclass Bayes gaps characterize the full infinite-class grid value.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:95:The full arbitrary infinite-class theorem is still not solved. Without product closedness or domination, finite-subclass Bayes gaps can miss a compactness/measurability gap.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:97:The domination theorem still needs a fully referee-ready statement of Borel versus completed-measure policy conventions.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:99:No matching lower bound is known for the power-tail upper rate. The \(qD\) hidden-terminal atom is useful but too weak for power tails.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:101:The result is not a scalar tail characterization. It is essentially a minimax value characterization.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:111:Rare histories break essential-sup tails: \(\beta(h)=1\) for all finite \(h\), while \(\sup_TV_T\le4\) and \(\Delta_T(H)=O(1/H)\).
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:113:Tiny-price blocks break raw delay tails because long delays can carry negligible value.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:115:Arbitrary infinite classes have real no-gap failures; finite-subclass control alone is insufficient.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:119:Write the finite/no-gap Bayes value theorem as the main characterization, and present \(\delta_T(H)\) only as a sufficient tail condition.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:123:Search for multi-scale or limited-leakage hidden-value constructions that accumulate regret without future spikes causing old quotes to trade.
iterations/iter_009/solver_attempt.md:125:Develop identifiable nondominated-class results separately from domination/product-compactness arguments.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:14:## Arbitrary known finite-horizon exogenous laws refute fixed-law tail necessity
working_notes/counterexamples.md:21:Why it matters: This subsumes iid-uniform and terminal-spike singleton examples: no nontrivial raw-delay or truncation-tail condition can be necessary under these quantifiers.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:34:Facts: The grid comparator value is \(V^G=1/2\), but the continuum comparator supremum is \(3/4\). Thus an exact finite-grid minimax identity using continuum \(V\) or a continuum Bayes envelope is off by \(1/4\).
working_notes/counterexamples.md:35:Why it matters: Finite-grid theorems must restrict learner actions, comparator, and Bayes predictive envelope to the same grid. Passing to \([0,1]\) requires a separate discretization error.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:39:## Randomization is necessary for minimax/Bayes identities
working_notes/counterexamples.md:66:Facts: Before terminal time the learner cannot distinguish worlds. Per early quote \(a\), \(g_i(a)=a\Pr(Y_i>a)\) has \(V_L=1/2\), \(V_H=3/4\), while the best Bayes action under a uniform prior has value \(1/2\). Bayes gap is \(1/8\) per early round.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:72:Construction: Horizon \(T=D+1\). Two worlds share \(P_1=\cdots=P_D=0\). At terminal time, world \(L\) uses \((1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[1/2,5/8]\), while world \(H\) uses \((1-q)\delta_0+q\,{\rm Unif}[3/4,7/8]\). For infinite-process tail calculations, set \(P_s=0\) for \(s>T\).
working_notes/counterexamples.md:78:Why it matters: This is the cleanest quantitative lower-bound atom linking valuable delayed tail mass and indistinguishability.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:79:Status: Valid finite-horizon minimax lower bound. It is not a singleton known-law obstruction, and by itself it does not match power-tail upper rates.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:82:## One-block power-tail lower-bound weakness
working_notes/counterexamples.md:83:Construction idea: Use the q-scaled atom under a nonincreasing tail envelope \(B\) by choosing \(q\le B(D)\).
working_notes/counterexamples.md:106:Thus zero quoting has bounded expected regret even though raw essential-sup tails are maximally bad.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:132:Status: Valid infinite-process/raw-\(\beta\) counterexample. For finite-horizon \(\beta_T\), state the result as failure of any uniform raw-tail necessity rather than \(\beta_T(h)=1\) for all \(h\).
working_notes/counterexamples.md:148:Why it matters: The finite-subclass Bayes modulus is not automatically a full infinite-class characterization. It characterizes the product-closure relaxation unless the attainable reward set is product closed.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:150:Iteration 8 refinement: payoff-weighted reward-measure domination fails in the non-Borel label example because the relevant class contains uncountably many singular Dirac-type prefix/reward measures. Thus it remains a stress test for any unrestricted infinite-class theorem. Iteration 9 found no natural Borel-label version; identifiable Borel and countable-label variants are easy by direct decoding.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:153:## Weak/Feller continuity is not a no-gap condition by itself
working_notes/counterexamples.md:156:Why it matters: Failure of product closedness and high regret are distinct. Identifiability may solve some nondominated classes, but it is a different mechanism from compactness and does not justify a general no-gap theorem.
working_notes/counterexamples.md:157:Status: Valid stress test against weak-continuity-only no-gap claims; not a lower-bound example by itself.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:4:Attempt: Prove that sublinear regret is possible only if the essential-sup hitting tail \(\beta(h)\to0\).
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:20:Attempt: Find any non-vacuous tail, truncation, or delay condition necessary for sublinear regret when the learner knows the finite-horizon exogenous law.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:39:Failure: Regret compares to \(\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)\). With iid block types, the best fixed expected comparator can be the same Bayes action available to the learner, so per-block realized optimal-action gaps do not add.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:43:## One-block q-scaled lower bound as a matching power-tail rate
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:44:Attempt: Use the q-scaled hidden-terminal atom to match the truncation upper rate under a tail envelope \(B(h)\), by setting \(q\approx B(D)\).
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:46:Lesson: Matching minimax lower rates need a construction that accumulates hidden valuable reward across scales without iid comparator collapse or one-reveal learning. Possible routes are multi-scale hidden states, limited-information terminal reveals, or Le Cam/Fano-style families.
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:50:Attempt: Declare the finite-horizon exogenous minimax problem solved by the least-favorable Bayes predictability gap
working_notes/failed_attempts.md:61:Lesson: \(\Psi\) is useful lower-bound/Bayes-value language, but exact characterization must first be proved on finite \(\Theta\) and finite \(G\) with grid-restricted comparator \(V_\theta^G\). The continuum passage is approximate and must carry \(O(T/K)\)-type errors.
