iterations/iter_003/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:3:The explorer’s main finite-horizon minimax claim survives: the q-scaled hidden-terminal pair gives a clean \(\Omega(qD)\) lower-bound atom under unknown-class, exogenous-price quantifiers. I do not see a fatal flaw in the Bayes-gap argument.
iterations/iter_003/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:5:The weak point is not the lower bound itself, but the tail-profile interpretation. The \(\beta\) computation needs an explicit post-horizon extension or a finite-horizon version of \(\beta\), and the construction is still far from a matching power-tail lower bound.
iterations/iter_003/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:9:- **Missing assumption:** The construction specifies only \(P_{1:T}\), but \(\beta(h)\) is originally an infinite-process tail. To claim \(\beta(h)=q\) for \(h<D\) and \(0\) for \(h\ge D\), one must define \(P_s=0\) for all \(s>T\), or explicitly introduce a finite-horizon \(\beta_T\).
iterations/iter_003/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:11:- **Missing assumption:** The lower bound requires the learner not to know whether the world is \(L\) or \(H\). As a singleton known-law instance, each world has zero regret by the known-law oracle. Any statement like “q-tail mass forces regret” must say “for a common learner over an unknown class.”
iterations/iter_003/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:13:- **Plausible but incomplete:** The envelope claim “if \(q\le B(D)\), then \(\beta(h)\le B(h)\)” assumes \(B\) is nonincreasing and that the relevant tail is the finite-horizon/absorbing extension tail. Without monotonicity, the right condition is \(q\le \inf_{h<D} B(h)\).
iterations/iter_003/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:21:- **Worth pursuing:** The general Bayes-gap lemma is strong and reusable. The proof only needs observational identity before the terminal reveal and the comparator being world-specific:
iterations/iter_003/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:34:- **Power-tail stress test:** With \(q=D^{-\alpha}\), the lower bound is \(D^{1-\alpha}/8\). For \(\alpha\ge1\), this is not a growing regret lower bound, so it cannot support a minimax rate theorem by itself.
iterations/iter_003/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:36:- **Repeated-block stress test:** Repetition remains dangerous. Iid block types can make the expected fixed comparator collapse to the Bayes action; common global type can be learned after the first reveal. The explorer correctly flags this.
iterations/iter_003/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:59:- The negative message that this atom alone does not match the truncation upper rate for power tails.
